You are building the production backend for a multi-tenant restaurant POS/CRM SaaS ("v2"). The backend lives at the monorepo root (~/v2), the React client lives in ~/v2/client. Do not modify anything under client/ — treat its src/types/*.ts and src/constants/*.ts as the authoritative contract for what you must build. Read every file in client/src/types/ and client/src/constants/ before writing any backend code; they define every shape, enum and permission string you need to match exactly (field names, optionality, casing).

STACK (non-negotiable)
- Runtime: Bun (not Node). Use Bun's own APIs wherever one exists: Bun.serve is not needed (Hono's bun adapter handles it), but use Bun.password for hashing if you ever need it outside better-auth, Bun's built-in .env loading, Bun's built-in test runner (bun test) for any tests, Bun's built-in SQLite/file APIs are NOT relevant here (we use MongoDB), but prefer Bun built-ins over npm packages for anything they cover (password hashing, file I/O, env, testing, fetch).
- Web framework: Hono. Use Hono's own built-in middleware (hono/cors, hono/logger, hono/secure-headers, hono/compress, hono/timing, hono/validator or Hono's zValidator pattern, hono/trailing-slash, etc.) before reaching for a third-party package. Only add a third-party library when Hono and Bun genuinely have no equivalent.
- Database/ORM: Mongoose (already installed, v9.x — this is a recent major version, do NOT write Mongoose 6/7-style code; check what changed in Mongoose 8→9, e.g. query defaults, strictQuery behavior, removed callback support).
- Auth: better-auth is already installed (v1.7.x) and is clearly the intended auth layer (the frontend already has DeviceSession, WebSession, MyInvitation, Membership types that match better-auth's multi-session plugin shape almost exactly). Use better-auth's Mongoose/MongoDB adapter and its multi-session, organization-style patterns rather than hand-rolling sessions/JWTs. Do not use a deprecated or legacy better-auth API — check the current (2026) better-auth docs for the Mongoose adapter setup, since this package moves fast.
- Validation: Zod (already a frontend dependency — see constants/pin_schema.ts for the exact pattern/style already used: z.object with .refine()). Mirror that style on the backend for consistency. Use Hono's official Zod validator middleware (@hono/zod-validator) rather than writing manual parsing in every controller.
- Language: TypeScript, strict mode. peerDependency already pins typescript ^7 — this is a new major TS version, so check what's changed (e.g. stricter defaults, any renamed compiler flags) before assuming TS 5.x behavior.

DO A FRESH "2026" DOCUMENTATION PASS BEFORE WRITING CODE
Your training data is stale relative to this stack's current state. Before implementing, explicitly fetch and read current documentation for: Hono (routing, middleware, validator, context typing, the Bun adapter), Bun (serve/adapter usage with Hono, built-in APIs you plan to use), better-auth (Mongoose/MongoDB adapter, multi-session plugin, email+password + any invite/organization patterns it offers), and Mongoose 9.x migration notes (anything deprecated/removed since 7/8). Do not write a single model or route based on memory of older versions of these libraries — verify current method names, options and recommended patterns first. If something you remember as idiomatic is now deprecated or replaced, use the replacement. Zero deprecated APIs anywhere in the output — if a library flags something deprecated in its current docs, don't use it, even if it still technically works.

PROJECT STRUCTURE (required)
src/
  models/        one Mongoose model per file, default export, matches naming style below
  controllers/    request handlers, thin — call into models/services, do the actual logic here
  routes/         Hono routers, one per resource, mounted under /api/v1
  middlewares/    auth guard, permission guard, error handler, request logging, branch-scope resolver
  constants/      permission catalog, enums, mirrored 1:1 from the frontend's constants/*.ts
  validations/    Zod schemas per resource, used by the Hono zod-validator middleware
index.ts          Hono app assembly + Bun server start

Follow the frontend's existing file conventions: default export per file, one concern per file, snake_case filenames (e.g. menu_item.ts, order_status.ts) to match client/src/types and client/src/constants exactly in name and casing where the concept is shared.

DOMAIN MODEL — derived directly from client/src/types, implement Mongoose schemas that produce API responses matching these shapes exactly (same field names, same optionality):

Branch: _id, name, address?, area?, city?, phone?, isActive?, archivedAt?
  — scoped per restaurant (tenant). Soft-delete via archivedAt, not hard delete.

Customer: _id, name, phone (normalized +923XXXXXXXXX), altPhone?, addresses?: string[], area?, notes?, tags: string[], birthday? (YYYY-MM-DD), khataBalance: number, orderCount: number, totalSpent: number, lastOrderAt?, lastOrder? (LastOrder shape — look at types/last_order.ts), source?: 'staff'|'qr'|'import', whatsappOptIn?: { at, source }, createdAt
  — khataBalance/orderCount/totalSpent/lastOrder are derived/denormalized fields, kept in sync from Order and KhataEntry writes (decide: recompute on write vs. stored aggregate — stored aggregate updated transactionally is preferred for list-page performance).
  — list responses omit addresses/notes (per the doc comment "Not sent in list responses"); profile/detail responses include them. Implement this as a projection difference between list and detail controllers, not two different models.

Menu / MenuCategory / MenuItem / MenuSize / AddonGroup / AddonOption:
  GET /menu returns { categories: MenuCategory[], addonGroups: AddonGroup[], items: MenuItem[] } — categories have sortOrder; items have categoryId, sizes (embedded array of {_id,name,price}), addonGroupIds (references), soldOut (per-branch!) and hidden (global). Model soldOut carefully: it's "sold out in the active branch," so either embed branch-level soldOut flags or keep a separate per-branch stock-override collection — pick one and justify it based on how tables.ts/floor.ts model branch-scoped state.
  AddonGroup: min/max control required vs optional/single vs multi selection — enforce these bounds server-side when an order is placed (validate order items' chosen addons against the group's min/max and against addonGroupIds on the item).

Order: _id, dailyToken (resets to 1 daily, Pakistan time — implement with a per-branch-per-day counter, not a global one, and compute "day" in Asia/Karachi, not UTC), branchId, customerId?, customer? (OrderCustomer = pick _id/name/phone, embedded snapshot for list responses), type (OrderType = dine_in|takeaway|delivery), status (new|preparing|ready|out_for_delivery|cancelled|completed), items: OrderItem[] (itemId, name, sizeName?, addons?:{name,price}[], note?, price, qty — names/prices are snapshotted from the menu at order time, not live-joined), subtotal, discount, taxRate, tax, deliveryFee, total, paymentMethod? (empty until paid — tax is added when payment is recorded, per the doc comment, so compute tax at payment time not order-creation time), paymentStatus (paid|unpaid|khata), tableId?, tableName? (snapshotted), area?, address?, rider?, createdAt.
  Khata orders must write a KhataEntry (type 'order') and increment Customer.khataBalance atomically with the order's payment update — use a Mongoose session/transaction.

OrderItem/OrderStatus/OrderType/OrderCustomer: as above — OrderCustomer is a Pick<Customer,'_id'|'name'|'phone'>, implement as a lean projection, not a duplicated schema.

Reservation: _id, branchId, customerId, customer? (OrderCustomer snapshot), partySize, dateTime, status (pending|confirmed|seated|cancelled), tableId?, note?.

Table / FloorTable: Table{_id,name,section?,seats?,qrCode (random slug for /t/<code> public link),archived}. GET /tables/floor must return FloorTable extends Table with: orders (today's unfinished-or-finished-but-unpaid dine-in orders at that table — a derived query, not a stored field), requests (open guest 'waiter'|'bill' calls from the QR menu — a separate GuestRequest model, short-lived), booking (a Reservation within -30min/+90min window — derived query). Build this as one aggregation/controller, not a denormalized model.

Role / Permission: Role{_id,name,description?,key? (owner/manager/cashier/waiter/kitchen for presets),isPreset,permissions: Permission[],memberCount}. Permission is a closed string union — copy constants/permission_catalog.ts's exact permission strings (orders:view, orders:create, orders:update, orders:payment, orders:discount, orders:cancel, customers:view, customers:manage, customers:khata, customers:import, customers:delete, reservations:view, reservations:manage, tables:view, tables:manage, menu:view, menu:update, menu:stock, reports:view, reports:view_all_branches, staff:view, staff:invite, staff:manage, teams:manage, roles:manage, settings:manage, branches:manage, devices:manage, audit:view) into constants/permission_catalog.ts on the backend verbatim, and build the permission-check middleware against this exact list. Owner role implicitly has every permission (permissions: [] but isOwner bypasses checks entirely — see StaffMember.isOwner). memberCount is a derived count, not stored.

StaffMember: _id, userId, user:{_id,name,email,image}, roleIds: string[], branchScope ('all'|'list'), branchIds: string[], status ('active'|'suspended'), joinedAt, hasPin, pinLocked, isOwner, isPrimaryOwner.
  hasPin/pinLocked relate to POS PIN auth (see pin_schema.ts: 4-6 digits, reject repeated/sequential PINs — reimplement that exact weak-PIN check server-side too, don't trust client validation). pinLocked sets after N wrong attempts; separate from account suspension.

Membership: _id, status, roleNames: string[], isPrimaryOwner, restaurant:{_id,name,status:'active'|'deleting',deleteAfter?}. 'deleting' + deleteAfter implies soft-delete-with-grace-period for whole restaurants — implement a scheduled job (Bun's setInterval/cron equivalent, or a dedicated worker) that hard-deletes restaurants past deleteAfter.

Invitation/InvitationLookup/MyInvitation: Invitation{_id,channel:'email'|'phone',email?,phone?,name?,roleIds,branchScope,branchIds,status:'pending'|'accepted'|'revoked',expired (derived from expiresAt, not stored),expiresAt,invitedByName?,createdAt}. GET /invitations/lookup/:token is a public (unauthenticated) endpoint returning InvitationLookup with partially-masked contact — mask the email/phone server-side (e.g. j***@example.com), never send the raw invitations collection token's associated contact unmasked to an unauthenticated caller beyond what InvitationLookup specifies.

Settings: restaurantName, logo? (URL, or the frontend sends a data URL while unsaved — backend must accept and store an uploaded image, not just a URL string; implement file upload, e.g. to local disk or S3-compatible storage, with Hono's built-in body parsing for multipart), onboarded (first-run flag), taxSet, taxByPaymentMethod: Record<PaymentMethod,number> (PaymentMethod = cash|card|jazzcash|easypaisa|bank), whatsappTemplates: Record<TemplateKey,string> (check constants/template_keys.ts on the frontend for the exact key list), updatedAt?. One Settings doc per restaurant (tenant), not per branch.

Device/DeviceSession/PosState/PosPerson: Device{_id,name,branchId,settings:{idleLockSeconds,lockAfterOrder},lastSeenAt?,createdAt,current}. PosState is what a registered terminal's /pos endpoints return: device info, restaurant{name,logo?}, branch (Pick _id/name), actor (null until PIN-unlocked, then {name,membershipId}), pausedUntil (terminal-wide PIN lockout after repeated failures — separate from a single staff member's pinLocked). PosPerson is the unlock-picker list: membershipId,name,image?,roleNames,locked. Build POS auth as: device-level pairing/registration (once, via an authenticated web session) issuing a long-lived device credential, then PIN-based actor switching on top of that device session — the PIN never replaces the device's own auth, it just identifies which staff member is "acting" on an already-trusted terminal.

Team: _id,name,description?,branchId?,membershipIds: string[],archivedAt?.

KhataEntry: _id,type:'order'|'adjustment'|'payment'|'reversal'|'opening'|'merge',amount,orderToken?,reversedAt?,note?,by?:{name},balanceAfter,createdAt. balanceAfter must be computed transactionally at write time (not recomputed on read) — append-only ledger pattern, each entry recording the resulting balance.

DashboardStats/DashboardAnswer: aggregation endpoints (GET /dashboard) — sales, orderCount, byType (grouped by OrderType), cash/digital (split by paymentMethod category), notCollected (today's unpaid+khata orders), newCustomers, khataTotal, khataCustomers, perBranch (only when "All branches" is selected), slipping (top customers inactive 30+ days), slippingTotal. Build with Mongoose aggregation pipelines, not application-level loops over fetched documents.

AuditEntry: _id,action,actor:{name?,via:'web'|'pos'|'system'},target:{type,id?,label?},changes?:{before?,after?},at. Write an audit entry from a shared middleware/helper on every mutating controller (orders, customers, roles, staff, settings, devices, etc.) rather than duplicating logging logic per-controller.

PublicMenu: GET /public/t/:code is the only fully public, unauthenticated endpoint — returns a reduced/sanitized menu (no cost/internal fields) for the table scanned. Treat this route's rate-limiting and input validation (the :code param) especially carefully since it's open to the internet.

MULTI-TENANCY
Every tenant-scoped model (Branch, Customer, Order, Menu*, Role, StaffMember, Invitation, Settings, Device, Team, KhataEntry, AuditEntry) must carry a restaurantId and every query must be scoped by it — implement this as a middleware that resolves the current restaurant from the authenticated session/membership and either (a) injects restaurantId into every query via a Mongoose plugin/schema middleware, or (b) requires every controller to explicitly pass it. Pick (a) if Mongoose 9's current plugin API supports it cleanly post-research; it's less error-prone than trusting every controller to remember.

API CONTRACT
Base path /api/v1 (the frontend's axios instance already targets this with withCredentials: true — cookie-based auth, so better-auth's cookie session handling must be CORS-configured for the client's origin with credentials). Endpoints confirmed by the frontend's own doc comments — implement at least: GET /menu, GET /roles, GET /members, GET /tables/floor, GET /invitations, GET /invitations/lookup/:token, GET /teams, GET /devices, GET /dashboard, GET /audit, GET /public/t/:code, GET /me/sessions, GET /pos/people, plus the full CRUD/action set each resource implies (create/update orders, record payments, customers CRUD + import + khata entries, reservations CRUD, tables CRUD + QR regen, menu CRUD + stock toggle, roles CRUD, staff invite/accept/suspend/remove, settings update, devices register/update, teams CRUD, branches CRUD, POS unlock/lock/pin-set, auth sign-in/sign-up/sign-out via better-auth's own routes).

Build the permission-middleware so every route declares the exact permission string(s) it requires (from the shared constants/permission_catalog.ts), and the owner bypass is centralized in one place, not re-checked ad hoc per controller.

Give me the complete backend: all models, controllers, routes, middlewares, constants and validations, wired together in index.ts, with better-auth's Mongoose adapter configured, CORS set for credentialed requests, and a .env-driven Mongo connection string. No placeholder/TODO logic for anything listed above — implement it for real, including the transactional khata/payment logic and the per-branch daily token counter.